Sceva is introduced in Acts 19:14 as a Jewish "chief priest" (archiereus) whose seven sons worked as itinerant exorcists in Ephesus. Outside this single verse, nothing else is definitively known about him, and his name comes from Latin rather than Hebrew.

Who Was Sceva?
Because no high priest named Sceva appears in the historical records of the Jerusalem Temple, scholars view his title in a few ways:
A member of the priestly aristocracy: In the first century, "chief priest" was sometimes used broadly for prominent men belonging to high-priestly families.
A self-styled title for credibility: In Greco-Roman magical circles, Jewish priests were believed to possess secret knowledge of God's sacred name. Sceva—or his sons—may have claimed the title in a Diaspora city like Ephesus to boost their reputation and business as exorcists.

Head of a priestly division: He may have served as the leader of one of the 24 rotating priestly courses in Jerusalem before moving abroad.

Meaning and Etymology of the Name
The New Testament was written in Greek, where his name appears as Skeuas (Σκευᾶς).
Latin Origin (Primary Consensus): Most scholars trace Skeuas to the common Roman family name Scaeva, which means "left-handed". In ancient Roman culture, being left-handed carried connotations of being "ominous," "unlucky," or "untrustworthy" (the root of the English word sinister).
Greek Alternative: Some linguists note a resemblance to the Greek noun skeuos (σκεῦος), meaning "vessel," "utensil," or "implement".


