Is There a Hebrew Name?
Sceva does not have a confirmed Hebrew name because Sceva is a Latin/Roman name—a common practice among Jews living in the Greco-Roman world (similar to Saul also using the Roman name Paul).


Biblical Training.org
However, scholars and modern Hebrew translations handle it in two ways:
Modern Hebrew Transliteration: In Hebrew translations of the New Testament, the Greek name is simply sounded out letter-for-letter as סְקֵוָא (Skeva).
Proposed Hebrew Equivalents: Some older commentators speculated that Skeva was a Greek adaptation of a Hebrew name like Shekanyah (שְׁכַנְיָה, Shecaniah), meaning "Yahweh has dwelt", while the early church father Jerome suggested it related to the Hebrew word for "fox" (shu'al, שׁוּעָל), though neither theory has broad scholarly support.

