Understanding first-century Jewish exorcism and the magical culture of Ephesus helps explain why the seven sons of Sceva acted the way they did—and why their failure caused such a dramatic reaction across the city.
1. First-Century Jewish Exorcism Practices
In the first century, traveling Jewish exorcists were well known throughout the Roman Empire, and they had a strong reputation for effectiveness. Unlike Jesus or Paul, who cast out spirits by direct personal command, standard Jewish exorcism relied heavily on rituals, objects, and long strings of powerful names.
Stacking Names of Power: Ancient magical texts—such as the Greek Magical Papyri—show that exorcists treated names like passwords. To ensure a spell worked, practitioners would stack together the names of angels, patriarchs (Abraham, Isaac, Jacob), King Solomon, and sacred titles for God (like Iao or Sabaoth).
Adding "Jesus" to the Formula: To the sons of Sceva, Jesus was simply a newly discovered "power name." Seeing Paul's miracles, they tried to add "the Jesus whom Paul proclaims" to their existing repertoire as a technical formula, without having a personal relationship with Jesus.
Physical Objects and Incantations: First-century historian Josephus describes a Jewish exorcist named Eleazar who drew out a demon using a special ring containing a root prescribed by Solomon, reciting incantations while invoking Solomon's name.
2. The Cultural Background of Magic in Ephesus
Ephesus was the capital of the Roman province of Asia and one of the most famous centers for magic, astrology, and occult practices in the ancient world.
The Temple of Artemis: Ephesus housed the massive Temple of Artemis (one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World). The cult of Artemis was deeply tied to mystery religions, astrology, and protective spells.
"Ephesian Letters" (Ephesia Grammata): The city was so famous for sorcery that magic spells and amulets across the Mediterranean were literally called "Ephesian Letters." These were secret, supposedly untranslatable words written on parchment or metal and worn as charms to ward off evil spirits and bad luck.
High-Value Spellbooks: Because magic was a major industry in Ephesus, manuals containing secret incantations were prized possessions. When new believers publicly burned their scroll collections in Acts 19:19, the value was estimated at 50,000 pieces of silver—roughly equivalent to 50,000 days' wages for a common laborer.
How These Two Worlds Collided
In a city obsessed with finding the strongest secret spell, the sons of Sceva represented the ultimate "power combo": sons of a Jewish chief priest using the latest miracle-working name in town. When the evil spirit exposed them as frauds and overpowered all seven men, it shattered the local belief that spiritual power could be bought, memorized, or manipulated like magic.
