Jesus and Paul rejected magical formulas, rituals, and physical props, instead casting out spirits through direct, personal authority. While Jesus commanded demons in His own sovereign name, Paul acted as an authorized representative commanding them directly in the name of Jesus Christ.
How Their Approach Differed from Ancient Exorcists
Feature	Ancient Exorcists (e.g., Sons of Sceva)	Jesus	Paul
Source of Power	Secret knowledge, rituals, and "power names"	His own divine identity and inherent authority	Delegated authority from Jesus Christ
Method	Long incantations, stacking divine/angelic names	Brief, direct commands (e.g., "Be silent, and come out")	Direct command in Jesus' name (e.g., Acts 16:18)
Physical Props	Rings, roots, smoke, amulets, and spellbooks	None—purely His spoken word	None for exorcisms (handkerchiefs in Acts 19:12 were God's sovereign work, not magical objects)
Relationship	Transactional—treating names like passwords	Relational—demons recognized Him as the Son of God	Relational—a commissioned apostle acting in faith
1. Jesus: Sovereign, Inherent Authority
In the Gospels, observers were consistently astonished not just that Jesus cast out demons, but how He did it (e.g., Mark 1:27).
No Appeal to a Higher Power: Unlike Jewish exorcists who prayed to God or invoked Solomon, Jesus spoke on His own authority: "I command you, come out of him" (Mark 9:25).
Zero Rituals: He never used charms, potions, or lengthy prayers to force a spirit out. His word alone was decisive.
Immediate Recognition: Evil spirits immediately recognized Jesus' identity and authority without needing to be interrogated for their secret names (with the exception of "Legion" in Mark 5, which revealed the scale of the oppression rather than acting as a magical lever).
2. Paul: Delegated, Relational Authority
Paul followed Jesus' model of simplicity, acting strictly as an ambassador rather than a magician.
Direct Command in Jesus' Name: In Acts 16:18, when troubled by a spirit of divination in Philippi, Paul simply turned and said, "I command you in the name of Jesus Christ to come out of her," and it left immediately.
Personal Allegiance Over Formula: For Paul, invoking "the name of Jesus" was not reciting a magic word—it meant speaking on behalf of a living Lord he knew and served. That is why the demon in Ephesus respected Paul's authority while rejecting the sons of Sceva, who tried to borrow the name without belonging to Jesus.
